  notifications outlive it.
  [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mC. Derived list + persisted delivery ledger.[0m The visible list is computed from [38;5;153m(tasks, now)[39m as in A. Alongside it, a
  small persisted ledger of keys — [38;5;153m(taskId, kind)[39m → [38;5;153m{deliveredAt, readAt}[39m — whose only jobs are suppressing duplicate OS
  toasts and tracking read state.
  [3mWins when[0m you want A's freedom from drift plus B's correct once-only delivery. Deleting or completing a task removes
  its notification for free, because the list was never stored. Unread counts and toast-once both come from the ledger.
  The ledger is a keyed set, not a parallel record list, so there's much less to keep in sync.
  [3mCosts:[0m no history for deleted or completed tasks — once a task is gone, its notification is gone. Two concepts instead
  of one.
  [3mComplexity: moderate, but less than B.[0m

  [1mMy recommendation: C.[0m B's history is the only thing C gives up, and in a single-user local app the history of "your
  own task went overdue and then you completed it" has close to zero value — while the bug class B invites (notification
  list drifting out of sync with the task list, orphans, double-raises on reload) is the most likely way this thing
  breaks. C makes the expensive-to-get-right part (what you see) derived and therefore always correct, and confines
  persistence to the one question derivation genuinely cannot answer: [3mhave I already told you this?[0m

  If you later add a backend and watched-task notifications, C does need revisiting — cross-user events are genuinely
  events and want B's log. That's a real future cost, and I still think it's the right trade now rather than paying for
  B's machinery on spec.
